#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c70e801-fd99-586e-ad50-fca199a9d991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281802} {:answer/id #uuid "99578261-0e54-5ddf-9cbe-2131732d1331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283022} {:answer/id #uuid "b33f1a06-1ff7-5054-9b68-11c4a5d9ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283244} {:answer/id #uuid "cacc8365-ba97-5758-a9a1-2e6465cbb4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283463} {:answer/id #uuid "fca7c681-e646-5b9c-9404-16ca07e77a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33f1a06-1ff7-5054-9b68-11c4a5d9ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283244} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67bd542-ad74-5a89-8312-e4d33f763205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a86ebeb5-18d1-5f84-a46f-2e3c446ad259" :question/math-academy-id "q-165433" :question/problem "![](images/61/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "998e8071-e594-5e1a-94c2-3fceddba078d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "998e8071-e594-5e1a-94c2-3fceddba078d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291298} :answer-field/id #uuid "06640f46-fcae-53ff-a063-613635742b48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2c4d5ba-f8b4-53aa-9498-734e5ac94d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-251799" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d589816d2368a450c0b2bbc1d3c6323b09b9d6dc5272f67a505b80f0688b16ae.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-3,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,6),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7+3)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =2(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =2(y+2) \\\\ 9 & =2(y+2) \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =y+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096708 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52486476-6056-55af-9404-0c1d6b9322c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52486476-6056-55af-9404-0c1d6b9322c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529777} :answer-field/id #uuid "316c9be3-2f1d-573d-ad2e-1f7c5d034777" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530255} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd34fb19-51be-53a6-9439-14ce74d4d526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd34fb19-51be-53a6-9439-14ce74d4d526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530207} :answer-field/id #uuid "e607ce28-3784-5cd7-a847-51fef16dd3b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99019613-302f-550e-842d-827f9008365f" :question/math-academy-id "q-251821" :question/problem "![](images/72/72fdce3ebf5cbdb9e0b06e88e9fd5a519afdd79faedefd0ec24d003a8bc6e557.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-4,3)$ and passes through the point $(-3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-4,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}=4p(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-3,2),$ we substitute $x=-3$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-3)^{2} & =4p(-3+4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(1) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+3 \\\\ y & =1,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $5$ and  $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "544b7ae8-40b2-5ec8-83d6-d19730e6b5a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403705} {:answer/id #uuid "2316f0bc-0936-5acf-bd1b-fe6117ca3155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403706} {:answer/id #uuid "1bacfa54-8104-51a9-b2bd-c3c2a3c703ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403707} {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403708} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd38f7-11c0-5b67-b2ce-d02618e9c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019cfb67-fa2a-5255-b2d3-9c68213d29fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403708} :answer-field/id #uuid "199aaf87-46d2-5438-b5f3-ee557ec29887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166cdda3-01a4-5828-9800-429450a46515" :question/math-academy-id "q-73832" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e79a3a7c2fce5a3e3ec7d838ebe1327dcc5452168f499fad05eea85a08e8f8b0.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(2,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $0$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096706 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781aff63-be25-5dd9-a51d-fd41b763b3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346404} {:answer/id #uuid "86da7198-114b-5a34-b00b-6bfb9d90ae08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346521} {:answer/id #uuid "8b445de7-b798-5c9d-80fe-4e38cff789ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346556} {:answer/id #uuid "92de7067-16c8-564e-b4a0-1c6307b5d500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ and $-\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346637} {:answer/id #uuid "da9e8b39-ca80-5e20-a05d-52cf6b3dd6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "781aff63-be25-5dd9-a51d-fd41b763b3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346404} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b9448a-a274-5dad-828f-e13b7bb51e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3a199da-f774-5b31-869b-a8666e40a01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79252" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa95f5a4629b2f97943db55125f91d76de962534be820fe70112132e5fddaaec.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $-3.$"}]}
